WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:03.200
Text on an ivory background, read aloud: "Every focus app has a skip button." The word "skip" is struck through.

00:03.200 --> 00:07.200
The CodeLock dashboard on Windows. A pointer clicks "15m" and a real 15-minute countdown starts. Narration and caption: "CodeLock doesn't. Start a focus block, and get to work."

00:07.200 --> 00:09.200
Fifteen minutes later, labelled on screen. The same timer, now in its last seconds.

00:09.200 --> 00:12.200
The lock takes over the whole screen with a programming problem, Two Sum. "When the time is up, the screen locks."

00:12.200 --> 00:14.867
A first attempt is typed, sped up five times. "To get back in, solve a coding problem."

00:14.867 --> 00:19.400
The pointer clicks Submit. An arrow points to the real verdict: Wrong answer, 2 of 5 tests passed. "A wrong answer keeps you locked."

00:19.400 --> 00:24.067
The fix is typed, sped up five times, and submitted. "Fix it, and pass every test, fast enough."

00:24.067 --> 00:27.067
The app's result, held as a still: Accepted, 5 of 5 tests passed, within the 162 ms speed budget.

00:27.067 --> 00:30.067
The lock lets go: "You solved Two Sum without a hint." "And you're back to work."

00:30.067 --> 00:33.733
The lion mark. "CodeLock. Earn your next break." See how it works at tommydeleon.com. Solo project, Windows desktop app, not released.
